//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="shadowed\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.resolve.ambient, diag.sev.warn
//! warns: W0304
//!
//! s157 (wolf-lang#44, wolf-std F-0047) — `[conf.resolve.ambient]` at
//! a CALL site. `shadow_prelude.lu` pins the lint; this pins the
//! semantics the lint is advisory about, on the ambient name that
//! found the bug. Until this pin the compiler answered two different
//! questions about the same name: `resolve_name` took the declaration
//! (that is what W0304 is warning about), while the call path
//! re-derived dispatch from the spelled name and typed `read_line()`
//! against the AMBIENT signature — `str ! {eof, io, utf8}` — so a
//! shadowing declaration was both warned about and ignored, on both
//! compiler tiers. lupin's half of F-0047 was the mirror image: it
//! resolved to the ambient `read_line` and recursed forever. The
//! declaration wins now, and the three lanes print one line.
//!
//! `assert` stays unshadowable — `[conf.trap.assert]`'s carve-out is
//! the only one, and it is a primitive, not library surface.

fn read_line() -> str {
    "shadowed"
}

fn main() -> !int {
    print(read_line())
    0
}
